Message-ID: <199705142120.QAA08412@stommel.tamu.edu>
Date: Wed, 14 May 1997 16:20:44 -0500 (CDT)
From: Richard Henderson <richard@stommel.tamu.edu>
Subject: Re: log(-1) raises SIGFPE
In-Reply-To: <199705121909.QAA14014@hoggar.fisica.ufpr.br>

> Why isn't -mieee the default?

(A) It isn't for Digital Unix either, so in theory people building
    packages for Alpha know to expect it.

(B) It is a noticable performance hit; the pipeline stalls while
    instructions are determining whether they will fault for 
    software completion.

(C) Not very many programs actually care about non-finite arithmetic.
    Sure, so you give them a NaN, but they can't deal with that any
    better than if you just went ahead and faulted.

    There are exceptions, but that is why -mieee is there as opposed
    to be missing entirely.

r~

